  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1224 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nPattern remains consistent on Sunday, with high pressure overhead and \nsteady southwest flow bringing monsoon moisture into our mountains. \nThe model variation Sunday-Monday largely stems from subtle shifts \nin the position of the monsoon plume, though as of now model \nconsensus continues to place the best moisture heading into our \nsouthern mountains and plains. Meanwhile, the north and eastern \nportions of our CWA remain drier, with temperatures still well above \naverage. Highs will be in the high-90s over the plains and in the \n80s in the valleys. \n\nLooking further into next week, model consensus has improved a bit \nsince yesterday, though there is still some variation. An upper low \nand associated trough will move inland from the Pacific NW, sending \na shortwave across our area. The high pressure will remain over our \nregion, but the slight change in the flow, and some enhanced \nsynoptic forcing, will give us a better monsoon tap and a greater \nspread of showers and thunderstorms. While most of the activity will \nremain over the mountains, some scattered POPs have spread to the \nplains as well. Increased storm coverage and intensity will lead to \na greater risk of flash flooding over the burn scars. Meanwhile, \ntemps remain above average, though we may cool off a few degrees \nthanks to some additional cloud cover.",
